00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:09.040
Okay, let's pop the hood on the sufficient context agent mechanism. The agent does not rely on a

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:15.120
single retrieval pass followed by generation. Instead, it evaluates whether the collected passages

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.480
contain sufficient grounding for every hop in a multi-source question. When the check fails,

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.920
it issues a new targeted search, repeating until the context passes the sufficiency test.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.540
This adds latency, but produces the measured 34% factuality gain on exactly the queries where

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:38.660
ordinary retrieval augmented generation pipelines hallucinate. So when should you actually reach for

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:44.800
this versus a standard retrieval stack? Use it when the task involves chained facts across documents

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:51.220
and the cost of an incorrect answer is high. For simpler, single-hop lookups, the added loop is

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:56.840
unnecessary overhead. Tomorrow, keep an eye on any updates to the export control directive

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.740
and the first community benchmarks for diffusion Gemma against standard Gemma 4 checkpoints.

